.TH VOIX 1 "October 2026" "Voix 4.14.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
voix \- a Privilege Policy Enforcement Runtime
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B voix
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
[\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Voix is a Privilege Policy Enforcement Runtime for Unix-like operating systems. It is architected as a policy-driven execution system that can provide \fBsudo\fR- or \fBdoas\fR-like workflows, but it is not a drop-in replacement and does not implement their full command-line surface. Voix uses Pluggable Authentication Modules (PAM) for authentication and a YAML-based configuration file for defining access rules.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-C, \-\-config \fIFILE\fR
Use the specified file as the configuration source (default: /etc/voix.conf). The path must be absolute and every component must be root-owned, free of symlinks, and not group/world writable. The final file is re-checked on the opened descriptor.
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-check-config
Validate the configuration file and exit. Also runs semantic policy linting (empty ACL, unconfined root target, redundant rules, open permits, missing blocklist).
.TP
.B \-E, \-\-preserve-env
Accepted for compatibility but has no effect. Environment preservation is enabled by a \fIkeepenv\fR rule option or a profile with \fIscrub_environment: false\fR; the flag does not add to it.
.TP
.B \-H, \-\-home
Set HOME to the target user's home directory. Without this flag, HOME is not set to the target's home; it is present only if it survived sanitization.
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-login
Execute the command via the target's login shell. With a named command the request stays brokered and the catastrophic-command gate applies to that command; Voix does not inspect what the command does after the shell starts. Used bare, it opens an interactive shell as the target using the target's passwd shell (fallback /bin/sh) if that shell is absent from \fIsecurity.blocklist\fR. This option forces HOME and SHELL to the target's values, even when the rule grants \fIkeepenv\fR or the target is unconfined.
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-list
List the permit rules that apply to the current user. Evaluation follows runtime first-match semantics: a permit fully covered by an earlier deny is omitted, and a partially covered permit is marked "(partially restricted by an earlier deny)". A rule without a command is shown as "(all commands)". This is not a command-by-command prediction.
.TP
.B \-n
Non-interactive mode. A valid persisted ticket is reused if the matched rule grants \fIpersist\fR; otherwise Voix fails rather than prompting. \-n can never trigger a password prompt.
.TP
.B \-s
Execute the target account's passwd shell as the target, falling back to /bin/sh when the account has no shell. The invoking user's $SHELL is never consulted, because it is caller-controlled and must not influence a privilege transition. Use \-i for login-shell semantics.
.TP
.B \-u \fIUSER\fR
Execute command as the specified target user (default: root). Requires an explicit target rule in the ACL for non-root users. There is no passwordless-target bypass: the caller must authenticate unless the rule grants trust/nopass, the caller is root, or a valid persisted ticket exists.
.TP
.B \-k
Invalidate all persisted authentication tickets for the invoking user (see the \fIpersist\fR rule option). Works standalone without a command.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Show the version of this artifact.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show the help message.
.TP
.B \-\-run-tests
Run the built-in test suite and exit. Available only in builds configured with testing enabled (\fBBUILD_TESTING=ON\fR); in Release builds the harness is compiled out and the flag reports that tests are disabled.
.SH EXIT CODES
Voix uses stable values based on \fBsysexits(3)\fR where an appropriate code
exists. A command's own exit status is propagated verbatim, so the executed
program can also produce any of these values.
.TP
.B 0
The command ran successfully (or listing/validation succeeded).
.TP
.B 1
Authentication failed or a generic runtime check failed.
.TP
.B 64
Invalid command line: unknown flag, missing command, or bad \fB\-u\fR target.
.TP
.B 70
Internal invariant failure (fork, waitpid, signal setup).
.TP
.B 77
Policy denied the request, an unsafe executable was refused, or a destructive-command gate blocked it.
.TP
.B 78
Configuration could not be loaded, parsed, or validated.
.TP
.B 126
The command was found but could not be executed.
.TP
.B 127
The command could not be found.
.PP
Otherwise, the exit status of the executed command is propagated verbatim.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The Voix configuration file, located at \fI/etc/voix.conf\fR, is a YAML file that defines rules for command execution. Rules support per-rule options (\fItrust\fR/\fInopass\fR, \fIkeepenv\fR, \fIpersist\fR, \fInolog\fR), argument wildcards, named security profiles, environment injection via \fIenv\fR lists, and an administrator blocklist of exact paths plus \fBregex:\fR patterns.
.PP
See \fIdocs/CONFIG.md\fR for details on the YAML format. Package upgrades never overwrite a live configuration; the shipped sample is installed as \fI/etc/voix.conf.new\fR instead.
.SH SECURITY MODEL
Voix applies a tiered hardening strategy based on the target user's privilege level.
.PP
.B Privileged targets
(root, package manager): retain full capabilities and unrestricted
syscall access, inherited file descriptors are not closed, and no resource
limits, capability drops, or seccomp filters are applied. Unconfined targets
also preserve the full inherited environment (including loader variables such
as LD_*). An explicit \fIprofile: privileged\fR still follows its
\fIpreserve_full_environment\fR setting, which is false in the shipped sample.
This ensures legitimate root operations (package manager hooks, process
management) work without restriction.
.PP
.B Non-privileged targets:
all capabilities are dropped, resource limits are enforced fail-closed
(RLIMIT_NOFILE, RLIMIT_NPROC, RLIMIT_CORE),
.B PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS
is always set (independently of seccomp), a seccomp syscall
blacklist is applied when enabled in configuration, the environment is
scrubbed (clearenv) and rebuilt from a safe whitelist with PATH overridden to
trusted directories unless the matched rule grants \fIkeepenv\fR or the
profile sets \fIscrub_environment: false\fR, a umask of
022 is forced, and inherited file descriptors other than the pinned
executable are closed. On the restricted tier the executable is moved to
fd 3 and descriptors 4+ are closed.
.PP
.B All targets:
signal handlers are reset to SIG_DFL, the umask is forced to 022,
and all identity resolution (UID/GID/supplementary groups/home/shell) happens
in the parent before fork(); the child performs no name-service lookups prior
to execution.
.PP
Every requested command \-\- bare name, relative path, or absolute path \-\-
is canonicalized and must resolve to a regular file owned by root with no
group/world write bits. The validated device/inode pair is re-checked on the
opened descriptor, which is preserved across descriptor scrubbing (moved to
fd 3 on the restricted tier) and executed with \fBfexecve(2)\fR. Replacing the
path after validation cannot change which file runs. ACL rules and exact
blocklist entries use the same canonical policy path, so merged-/usr aliases
match without collapsing multicall applets.
.SH CATASTROPHIC COMMAND DETECTION
Independent of any configuration, Voix blocks unequivocally destructive
operations in the command it is asked to execute: \fBrm -rf\fR variants
targeting / (including globs, long options, combined short flags and
cwd-relative paths that canonicalize to /),
\fBdd\fR writing to raw block devices (sd/hd/vd/nvme/mmcblk/device-mapper/
disk aliases or the detected root device), the entire \fBmkfs*\fR family,
\fBmkswap\fR, and the basename-matched tools fdisk, sfdisk, cfdisk, parted,
wipe, wipefs and shred. Blocked attempts are always audited in full,
regardless of any nolog rule option. The gate inspects only the directly
requested command; a permitted shell or interpreter can invoke these tools.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
Voix uses the Pluggable Authentication Modules (PAM) framework for authentication.
The service name is \fBvoix\fR, and its configuration can be found in \fI/etc/pam.d/voix\fR.
Account validation (\fBpam_acct_mgmt\fR) runs for the invoking user on every
invocation \-\- an expired or disabled caller is denied even under
trust/nopass rules. The target account is validated separately with its own
PAM handle; that check is not credential verification and is never treated as
proof that the target's password was checked. The credential check is skipped
only when the invoking user is root, the rule carries trust/nopass, or a valid
\fIpersist\fR ticket exists for the same caller/target UID pair and the rule
carries \fIpersist\fR.
.PP
Rules carrying the \fIpersist\fR option store an authentication timestamp for
15 minutes under \fI<sanctuary>/timestamp/<caller-uid>-<target-uid>\fR
(strictly permission-checked). Only a successful \fBpam_authenticate()\fR mints
or refreshes a ticket; trust/nopass/root skips and reused tickets never do.
\fB\-n\fR reuses a valid ticket but never prompts. Use
.B voix -k
to invalidate all of the invoking user's tickets immediately.
.PP
Rules carrying the \fInolog\fR option suppress audit records containing the
command text; an outcome-only summary remains in the audit trail.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
User-facing failures are printed as \fBvoix: error:\fR followed by an optional
\fBvoix: hint:\fR remediation line. Fatal errors are printed even when
\fIcore.suppress_stderr\fR is enabled.
.PP
Audit records are written to \fI/var/log/voix.log\fR and to \fBsyslog\fR
(facility \fBauthpriv\fR). Each line includes a timestamp, level, PID, and
escaped control characters. Security events carry a stable code after the
user, for example \fB[alice] [command.denied]\fR or
\fB[alice] [auth.failed]\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
Execute \fBwhoami\fR as root:
.IP
voix whoami
.PP
Execute \fBwhoami\fR as a specific user:
.IP
voix -u user whoami
.PP
List permit rules:
.IP
voix -l
.PP
Invalidate persisted authentication:
.IP
voix -k
.PP
Start an interactive shell ascension:
.IP
voix -s
